add(1002,'centroid-computation','Centroid from raw moments','A nonempty binary region has m00=4, m10=10 and m01=6. What is its centroid (u_c,v_c)?',[
('(2.5, 1.5).','Divide each first moment by m00.'),('(10, 6).','These are unnormalized first moments.'),('(1.5, 2.5).','This swaps horizontal and vertical components.'),('(40, 24).','This multiplies by area rather than dividing.')], 'The first moments divided by area give the average foreground coordinate.',typ='numerical',difficulty='medium',steps=['u_c=10/4=2.5.','v_c=6/4=1.5.'],formulas=[F_MOM])
add(1002,'moment-order','Moment order and translation','Which pair correctly gives the order of m21 and the purpose of central moments?',[
('Order 3; centering removes dependence on an overall coordinate translation.','The order is p+q and central coordinates move with the centroid.'),('Order 2; centering removes every possible change of scale.','The order is 3, and centering alone does not normalize scale.'),('Order 1; centering replaces every region by a circle.','Neither follows from the definition.'),('Order 3; centering guarantees that all second moments are zero.','Only first central moments vanish; second moments describe spread.')], 'Raw moment order is the sum of its exponents. Central moments describe the distribution around its centroid.',kind='equation')
add(1003,'central-identities','Central second-moment calculation','Let m00=4, m10=10, m01=6, m20=30, m02=14 and m11=17. What is J=[[μ20,μ11],[μ11,μ02]]?',[
('[[5,2],[2,5]].','μ20=30−25=5, μ02=14−9=5, μ11=17−15=2.'),('[[30,17],[17,14]].','This uses raw rather than central moments.'),('[[5,−2],[−2,5]].','The displayed coordinate-scatter convention has positive μ11 here.'),('[[20,11],[11,8]].','This subtracts first moments instead of their area-normalized products.')], 'The central identities subtract centroid-dependent terms from the raw moments.',typ='numerical',difficulty='medium',steps=['Compute μ20=30−10²/4=5.','Compute μ02=14−6²/4=5.','Compute μ11=17−10×6/4=2.','Place the moments using the displayed matrix convention.'],formulas=[F_CENT,F_ELL])
add(1003,'ellipse-eigenvalues','Equivalent ellipse semiaxes','For the displayed J convention, let λ1=100, λ2=25 and m00=25. Which equivalent filled-ellipse semiaxes and aspect ratio b/a result?',[
('a=4, b=2, b/a=0.5.','Apply 2√(λ/m00) to each eigenvalue.'),('a=2, b=1, b/a=0.5.','The common factor 2 is omitted.'),('a=8, b=4, b/a=0.5.','These are diameters, not the requested semiaxes.'),('a=4, b=2, b/a=2.','The aspect ratio has been inverted.')], 'The source expression gives semiaxes of the equal-moment filled ellipse. Its minor-to-major ratio is 0.5.',typ='numerical',difficulty='medium',steps=['a=2√(100/25)=4.','b=2√(25/25)=2.','b/a=2/4=0.5.'],formulas=[F_ELL])
add(1003,'ellipse-full-synthesis','Moment-to-orientation synthesis','For J=[[5,2],[2,5]] and m00=4, derive the equivalent ellipse’s major-axis direction and semiaxis lengths.',[
('Major axis along (1,1); a=√7 and b=√3.','Eigenvalues are 7 and 3, with largest eigenvector along (1,1).'),('Major axis along (1,−1); a=√7 and b=√3.','The direction (1,−1) belongs to eigenvalue 3, not 7.'),('Major axis along (1,1); a=7 and b=3.','The semiaxes require square roots and area normalization.'),('Major axis along (1,0); a=b=√5.','The off-diagonal coupling cannot be discarded.')], 'Solving det(J−λI)=0 gives (5−λ)²−4=0, hence λ=7,3. The major eigendirection is (1,1), modulo its sign. With m00=4, the semiaxes are √7 and √3.',typ='derivation',difficulty='hard',steps=['Expand the characteristic equation (5−λ)²−4=0.','Obtain λ1=7 and λ2=3.','For λ1=7, solve −2x+2y=0, giving y=x.','Use a=2√(7/4)=√7 and b=2√(3/4)=√3.'],formulas=[F_ELL],assumptions=['Use the displayed coordinate second-moment matrix, not a differently signed mechanical inertia tensor.'])
add(1003,'orientation-qualification','Orientation ambiguity','Which qualification is necessary for the printed orientation formula atan(v_y/v_x) using a major eigenvector?',[
('Use quadrant-aware atan2 when needed; an axis is modulo π, and equal eigenvalues give no unique major direction.','These address division, quadrant and degeneracy ambiguities.'),('Eigenvectors always have positive horizontal components, so no qualification is needed.','Their sign is arbitrary and the horizontal component can be zero.'),('The sign of an eigenvector identifies two physically different ellipse axes.','Opposite eigenvectors describe the same unoriented axis.'),('Equal eigenvalues imply one uniquely vertical major axis.','An isotropic second-moment matrix has no unique major direction.')], 'A robust interpretation uses atan2(v_y,v_x), treats opposite vectors as the same axis and recognizes isotropic degeneracy.',kind='equation',conflict='The source uses atan(v_y/v_x) without quadrant, zero-denominator or equal-eigenvalue qualifications.')
add(1003,'hu-normalization','Hu moment invariance','How should the lecture’s seven Hu descriptors be understood?',[
('They are combinations of normalized central moments through third order, with continuous-model invariances and numerical/discretization limits.','Normalization is required for scale invariance and digital estimates are approximate.'),('They are the seven raw pixel intensities closest to the centroid.','Hu descriptors are moment combinations, not selected samples.'),('Any seven unnormalized central moments are automatically scale invariant.','Centering removes translation but not scale.'),('They guarantee identical values for every pair of distinct shapes.','Shape descriptors are discriminative summaries, not universal identical outputs.')], 'Hu invariants use normalized central moments, not merely arbitrary unnormalized moments. Sampling and numerical precision affect practical invariance.',conflict='The slide attributes invariance to seven scalars from central moments without explicitly stating the scale normalization.')
add(1004,'undefined-moments','Missing code context','After defining only m00=blobs[3].moments.m00 and u20=blobs[3].moments.mu20, the excerpt forms np.array([[u20,u11],[u11,u02]]). In a fresh namespace with blobs and np defined, what happens?',[
('NameError at u11; u11 and u02 need explicit definitions before construction.','Those names are not defined by the shown assignments.'),('NumPy infers u11 and u02 from the shape of the nested list.','Undefined Python names are resolved before NumPy sees the values.'),('The missing values automatically become zero.','Python does not fill undefined variables with zero.'),('The array is built correctly because m00 contains all central moments.','m00 is one scalar, not assignments to u11/u02.')], 'The excerpt needs, for example, u11=blobs[3].moments.mu11 and u02=blobs[3].moments.mu02. Those are added context, not lines already present in the slide.',kind='code',conflict='The displayed J3 array references u11 and u02 without defining them in the excerpt.')
add(1004,'blob-table','Reading blob measurements','Read the actual row for blob 3: centroid approximately (365.7,218.2), area 5.61e3, aspect 0.749, orientation −87.6°. Which interpretation fits the plot?',[
('It is the top, nearly vertical target; its centroid is an image location and its minor-to-major ratio is about 0.749.','The row and green ellipse/box overlay agree.'),('It is the bottom target; its centroid is an a*b* color-space point.','The row is spatial and matches the top target.'),('Its area is 0.749 and its orientation is 5.61e3 degrees.','This swaps the table’s measurements.'),('Its aspect ratio proves that its two semiaxes are equal.','Equality would require a ratio of one.')], 'The actual table row maps to the top target. Its spatial centroid differs from the earlier color-cluster centers.',typ='diagram',kind='table',locator='Blob table row id 3 and adjacent centroid/box/ellipse figure.')
add([1001,1004],'instance-index','Connected labels versus blob indices','The top target is connected label 1 in the label image but blob index 3 in the blob table. What follows?',[
('Different routines can enumerate the same instances differently; IDs must be matched by evidence rather than assumed equal.','The actual outputs use different enumerations.'),('The top target must have moved physically between the two algorithms.','Different IDs alone do not imply motion.'),('The table must have exactly three objects because index 3 appears.','Zero-based index 3 can identify the fourth object.'),('Label 0 must be a foreground object because blob index 0 is one.','The connected-label convention assigns 0 to background; the blob list is different.')], 'Class IDs, connected labels and blob-list indices are distinct conventions. The top target’s correspondence is established by its position.',typ='diagram',locator='Top target label 1 on p1001 and table/plot blob 3 on p1004.')
add(1004,'contour-area','Fractional area qualification','The source reports blobs[3].area=5609.5 after earlier defining binary m00 as a pixel count. How can these statements be reconciled?',[
('A contour-based polygon area can be fractional; it is not identical to counting foreground pixels in a 0/1 raster.','The measurement conventions differ.'),('A binary mask must contain exactly half of a foreground pixel value.','Fractional contour area does not require a half-valued mask pixel.'),('The decimal proves that all moment formulas are invalid.','Different area estimators do not invalidate the formulas under their declared conventions.'),('Every pixel count is necessarily rounded down to end in .5.','Integer binary counts do not follow that rule.')], 'The blob API derives shape measurements from contours. Its continuous polygon area can have half-unit values, unlike a direct integer pixel sum.',kind='code',locator='Area 5609.5 and blob moment measurements.',conflict='The area-as-pixel-count discussion and fractional blob contour area use different conventions without an explicit distinction.')
add(1005,'chain-code','Boundary chain code','A contour starts at a known pixel and is encoded by directions E, E, NE, N. With horizontal/vertical step length 1, what is the travelled length and what information does the start provide?',[
('3+√2; the start anchors the directional sequence to an absolute image location.','There are three axial steps and one diagonal.'),('4; the start is unnecessary for any absolute reconstruction.','This treats diagonal length as one and discards the translation anchor.'),('2+2√2; the start determines the shape’s color class.','Only one step is diagonal, and the start is a position.'),('3+√2; the start determines all missing spectral color information.','Length is right, but chain coordinates do not determine color.')], 'Direction codes compactly encode a boundary path. Metric length depends on step geometry, while the initial pixel fixes its translation.',typ='numerical',difficulty='medium',steps=['Count three unit axial steps and one √2 diagonal step.','Sum to 3+√2.','Use the declared starting pixel to accumulate absolute coordinates.'])
add(1005,'circularity-comparison','Circle and square circularity','Using ρ=4πA/P², compare an ideal circle with an ideal square of any positive side length.',[
('Circle: 1; square: π/4≈0.785.','Substitution cancels their scale parameters.'),('Circle: π/4; square: 1.','These values are reversed.'),('Both: 1, because both are closed shapes.','Closure alone does not make perimeter minimal for a given area.'),('Their circularities depend linearly on their size.','Uniform scale cancels from A/P².')], 'For a circle A=πr²,P=2πr; for a square A=s²,P=4s. The resulting ratios are 1 and π/4.',typ='derivation',difficulty='medium',steps=['Substitute circle formulas to obtain 4π·πr²/(2πr)²=1.','Substitute square formulas to obtain 4πs²/(4s)²=π/4.'],formulas=[F_CIRC])
add(1005,'circularity-scale','Circularity under resizing','A shape is uniformly enlarged by a factor of 3 in the ideal Euclidean model. How do area, perimeter and circularity change?',[
('Area ×9, perimeter ×3, circularity unchanged.','The factors cancel as 9/3².'),('Area ×3, perimeter ×3, circularity divided by 3.','Area scales quadratically, not linearly.'),('Area ×9, perimeter ×9, circularity divided by 9.','Perimeter scales linearly.'),('Area ×9, perimeter ×3, circularity multiplied by 3.','The ratio cancels rather than gaining a factor.')], 'Circularity is dimensionless and scale invariant in the ideal model; finite raster estimates can differ after resampling.',typ='scenario',difficulty='medium',formulas=[F_CIRC])
add(1005,'radius-rotation','Radius signature and rotation','Why does comparing raw radius samples at identical contour indices not automatically give rotation-invariant shape matching?',[
('Rotation or a changed contour start can shift the signature’s phase; alignment or an invariant comparison is needed.','The same shape can generate a cyclically shifted ordered sequence.'),('All radii become zero whenever the object rotates.','Rotation preserves distances to the correspondingly rotated centroid.'),('A radius signature contains only area and has no ordering.','The plotted signature is an ordered function or sequence.'),('Dividing radii by their sum necessarily corrects every phase shift.','Scale normalization does not align the sequence.')], 'Centroid-to-boundary distances can support rotation-robust matching, but their ordering must be aligned or handled invariantly.',conflict='The slide suggests radius signatures distinguish shapes regardless of orientation without stating phase alignment.')
add(1006,'polar-code-index','Polar profile index mismatch','The code begins r,th=blobs[0].polar(), while the plot caption names blob 3. What can be concluded from the excerpt alone?',[
('The literal call uses blob 0; the caption conflicts and cannot establish that this call used blob 3.','The code index and caption disagree.'),('The call necessarily uses blob 3 because captions override Python indices.','Captions do not change execution.'),('Zero-based indexing makes blobs[0] the same object as blobs[3] in every four-blob list.','The indices identify different positions.'),('The function automatically changes its argument to the top target.','No such selection appears in the call.')], 'The source code/caption mismatch must be preserved rather than silently replacing the index.',kind='code',conflict='The polar call uses blobs[0], but the associated caption says radius and theta of blob 3.')
add(1006,'polar-normalization','Normalized radius and polar matching','Which interpretation of the shown r/r.sum() curves and polarmatch(1) call is appropriate?',[
('The normalization removes a common radius scale under consistent sampling; polarmatch compares profiles to blob 1 and returns similarity plus orientation offset.','These distinguish scale normalization from alignment.'),('r/r.sum() makes every radius sample equal and removes all shape information.','Normalization divides by one common sum and preserves relative variation.'),('polarmatch(1) means there is exactly one blob in the image.','The argument selects the reference blob index.'),('The curve’s horizontal axis is automatically physical time in seconds.','The displayed curve is sampled along a contour, not a time series.')], 'Radius normalization preserves relative shape variation. Profile matching can estimate a rotational offset; the function argument identifies the comparison target.',kind='code',locator='r/r.sum(), polarmatch(1), and the normalized profile plot.')
add(1006,'perimeter-data','Perimeter samples versus length','What is the distinction between blobs[3].perimeter.shape and blobs[3].perimeter_length?',[
('The former describes the coordinate-array dimensions; the latter is a scalar boundary-length measurement.','A sampled contour and its metric length are different objects.'),('Both necessarily return the same scalar pixel count.','Array shape is not a scalar Euclidean length.'),('The former is object area; the latter is object brightness.','Neither meaning matches the API properties.'),('The former gives color-cluster centers; the latter gives a class ID.','These are boundary properties, not clustering results.')], 'The contour contains coordinate samples, conventionally one point per column, while perimeter_length measures distances along the boundary.',kind='code')
add(1007,'detector-masks','Detection versus instance masks','The page illustrates a Faster R-CNN detector with boxes and labels but refers to pixel/instance segmentation. Which distinction is accurate for standard architectures?',[
('Faster R-CNN provides object detections; Mask R-CNN adds a mask-prediction branch for instance masks.','Bounding boxes are not per-pixel masks.'),('Every Faster R-CNN prediction is inherently a full-resolution semantic mask.','The standard detector output is boxes, labels and scores.'),('A bounding box exactly specifies which interior pixels belong to an object.','Boxes include background and do not delineate every object pixel.'),('Adding a mask branch removes the need to identify object instances.','The mask branch produces masks associated with detected instances.')], 'Standard Faster R-CNN detection must not be conflated with mask prediction. The inspected street figure supports boxes and labels, not complete pixel masks.',conflict='The slide describes Faster R-CNN in pixel-classification/instance-segmentation terms while showing detector boxes.')
add(1007,'coco-scope','Training-set class scope','What does the source’s reference to 80 COCO object classes establish?',[
('The depicted pretrained configuration uses a particular class vocabulary; 80 is not a universal limit of the Faster R-CNN architecture.','Class count is tied to training/configuration.'),('Every possible detector architecture must have exactly 80 output classes.','Architectures can be configured and trained for other vocabularies.'),('Every image must contain all 80 object types.','The vocabulary is not a claim about each image’s content.'),('The detector requires no learned parameters because the classes are named.','Pretrained classification still depends on learned parameters.')], 'The stated class vocabulary belongs to the training configuration and should not be universalized to all models or scenes.')
add(1008,'line-structure','Line feature utility','Compared with the original street scene, what does the inspected line drawing primarily retain?',[
('Selected geometric boundary structure, with much of the color and texture omitted.','This is the intended line representation.'),('Every original pixel value in a reversible encoding.','Line extraction discards appearance information.'),('Only semantic class names and no geometry.','The output consists of spatial line structure.'),('A guaranteed physical depth for every boundary.','A 2D line drawing alone does not supply metric depth.')], 'Straight boundaries are useful geometric features in many engineered environments, without implying all scenes or all useful boundaries are straight.',typ='diagram',locator='Original urban image and extracted line rendering.')
add(1009,'gradient-values','Gradient magnitude and direction','For I_u=3 and I_v=4 at one pixel, what are the Euclidean gradient magnitude and direction from the +u axis?',[
('Magnitude 5; direction atan2(4,3)≈53.13°.','Use the Euclidean norm and ordered vertical/horizontal arguments.'),('Magnitude 7; direction atan2(4,3)≈53.13°.','7 is the L1 norm, not the explicitly requested Euclidean norm.'),('Magnitude 5; direction atan2(3,4)≈36.87°.','The direction arguments are swapped.'),('Magnitude 25; direction 4/3 degrees.','25 is the squared norm and a ratio is not an angle in degrees.')], 'The theory slide uses √(I_u²+I_v²) and atan2(I_v,I_u).',typ='numerical',difficulty='medium',steps=['Compute √(9+16)=5.','Evaluate atan2(4,3)≈53.13°.'],formulas=[formula('M=√(I_u²+I_v²); θ=atan2(I_v,I_u)',{'I_u,I_v':'image intensity derivatives in horizontal/vertical directions','M':'Euclidean gradient magnitude','θ':'gradient direction from positive u-axis'},{'I_u,I_v,M':'intensity per pixel','θ':'radians or explicitly converted degrees'},['Nonzero gradient for a defined direction.'])])
add(1009,'smoothing-derivatives','Smoothing and derivative convolution','Why can the sequence D_u*(G*I) be represented as (D_u*G)*I under compatible convolution conventions?',[
('Convolution is associative, allowing a derivative-of-Gaussian filter to combine smoothing and differentiation.','This is the displayed rearrangement.'),('Convolution always removes every edge from an image.','Smoothing reduces noise but does not imply total edge removal.'),('Derivative filtering and smoothing are the same operation for all kernels.','They have distinct roles even if combined in one filter.'),('The equality permits arbitrary incompatible boundary padding without changing results.','Finite-image boundary conventions must be compatible.')], 'Associativity combines the filters in the ideal or consistently handled convolution model; implementation boundaries remain relevant.',kind='equation')
add(1010,'nonmaximum-direction','Canny nonmaximum suppression','To thin a broad edge response, along which direction does Canny compare the local gradient-magnitude maximum?',[
('Along the local gradient direction, across the edge.','This suppresses responses away from the ridge.'),('Only along the edge tangent, regardless of gradient.','That does not perform the stated cross-edge thinning.'),('Along the image diagonal at every pixel.','The comparison direction depends on the local gradient.'),('Across every image pixel globally to retain one maximum only.','The test is local rather than one global selection.')], 'Nonmaximum suppression retains local ridge peaks along the gradient direction, making the edge response thin.')
add(1010,'hysteresis-chain','Canny hysteresis reasoning','Use high threshold 10 and low threshold 5, with strong defined as ≥10 and weak as [5,10). A chain has strengths 12→8→6; an isolated pixel has strength 8. Which survives hysteresis?',[
('The connected 12,8,6 chain survives; the isolated 8 does not.','Weak pixels can connect through a weak chain to a strong seed.'),('Only 12 survives because all sub-high pixels are discarded.','This omits hysteresis connectivity.'),('Every 8 survives because all values above the low threshold are accepted.','Isolated weak responses need a strong connection.'),('Only the isolated 8 survives because connected responses are redundant.','This reverses the connectivity criterion.')], 'Hysteresis retains weak responses that are connected to strong evidence, not every response above the low threshold.',typ='scenario',difficulty='medium',assumptions=['The stated threshold equality convention and chain adjacency apply.'])
add(1011,'hough-normal','Hough polar parameters','In ρ=u cosθ+v sinθ, what is θ?',[
('The angle of the line’s unit normal from the +u axis.','The coefficients form a unit normal.'),('Always the direction angle along the line itself.','Line direction differs by a right angle.'),('The number of edgels voting for the line.','That is accumulator support, not an angle.'),('The distance from the image center to the first detected corner.','That is neither this angle nor the declared origin-based offset.')], 'The polar representation avoids an infinite-slope special case by using a normal angle and perpendicular offset.',kind='equation',formulas=[F_HOUGH])
add(1011,'hough-coordinate','Vertical line in Hough coordinates','For image coordinates u rightward and v downward, a vertical line u=6 is represented using θ=0. What is ρ?',[
('6.','cos0=1 and sin0=0, so ρ=u.'),('0.','This confuses the angle with the offset.'),('v, varying along the line.','At θ=0 the v coefficient is zero.'),('Undefined, because a vertical line has infinite slope.','Polar coordinates avoid that slope singularity.')], 'The equation reduces to ρ=u=6 for all points on the vertical line.',typ='numerical',steps=['Substitute cos0=1 and sin0=0.','Obtain ρ=6.'],formulas=[F_HOUGH])
add(1011,'hough-voting','Accumulator voting','How does a common straight line through several edgels appear in the Hough parameter space?',[
('Their candidate curves intersect near a common parameter bin, accumulating votes.','Each point votes for lines that could pass through it.'),('Every edgel votes only for its own image coordinate as a line parameter.','Image coordinates and line parameters are different spaces.'),('Collinear edgels necessarily erase one another’s votes.','Their support reinforces a common candidate.'),('The accumulator stores only original RGB intensities without geometry.','It stores support for line parameters.')], 'A peak indicates support shared by multiple image points, subject to discretization and threshold choices.',typ='diagram',locator='Point-to-curve and shared parameter intersection illustrations.')
add(1012,'hough-fivepoints','Five-point line support','Consider the depicted five-point layout idealized as (9,9),(39,9),(24,24),(9,39),(39,39). Which candidate has more exact point support: v=39 or u+v=48?',[
('u+v=48 has three points; v=39 has two.','The center and two opposing corners satisfy the diagonal.'),('v=39 has three points; u+v=48 has two.','The counts are reversed.'),('Both have five points because the layout is symmetric.','Symmetry does not put all points on either line.'),('Neither has any point because lines must pass through the image origin.','Hough lines can have nonzero offsets.')], 'The diagonal contains (39,9),(24,24),(9,39), while the lower horizontal contains (9,39),(39,39). Coordinates are an explicitly declared idealization of the figure.',typ='diagram',difficulty='medium',locator='Five-point image and candidate-line overlays.',assumptions=['Use the explicitly supplied idealized coordinates; do not infer exact coordinates from raster thickness.'])
add(1013,'church-code','Church edge-to-line pipeline','Which ordering matches the church code?',[
('Convert to integer grayscale → Gaussian blur → Canny edges → probabilistic Hough line segments.','This follows the displayed calls.'),('Probabilistic Hough → RGB k-means → Gaussian blur → Canny edges.','The source does not use this reversed/color-clustering chain.'),('Canny edges → raw-image threshold → blob centroids → SIFT descriptors.','These are not the shown operations.'),('Gaussian blur → SIFT matching → connected labels → Hough circles.','The code uses Canny and line segments, not this pipeline.')], 'The code smooths the grayscale input, computes an edge map and detects line segments supported by those edges.',kind='code',locator='church.to_int, GaussianBlur, Canny, Hough and lines_p calls.')
add(1013,'houghp-parameters','Line segment parameter roles','In lines_p(100,minlinelength=200,maxlinegap=5,seed=0), what do the 200 and 5 control?',[
('Minimum accepted segment length and maximum gap used to link segment evidence, in pixels.','These control segment geometry and linking.'),('Low and high grayscale thresholds for pixel classification.','Those are not these HoughP parameters.'),('Number of output classes and number of color channels.','This is geometric line detection, not color clustering.'),('Gaussian width and Gaussian standard deviation.','Those belong to the earlier smoothing call.')], 'The HoughP arguments constrain segment length and allowed interruption. They do not promise an exact number of returned lines.',kind='code')
add([1009,1013],'canny-norm','Theory versus Canny default','The theory page uses √(I_u²+I_v²). The shown cv.Canny call does not set L2gradient. With the documented default L2gradient=False, which qualification is correct?',[
('The implementation uses an L1 gradient magnitude approximation by default; Euclidean L2 requires explicitly enabling it.','This distinguishes the API default from the theory formula.'),('The call must use L2 because every Canny implementation has only that norm.','The documented parameter selects the norm.'),('L2gradient=False disables all gradient computation.','It changes the magnitude norm, not the existence of derivatives.'),('The Canny thresholds are therefore thresholds on original color labels.','They apply to edge strength, not color-class IDs.')], 'The mathematical presentation and the displayed API defaults are not identical. State the norm explicitly when reproducing numerical behavior.',kind='code',conflict='The theory slide uses Euclidean gradient magnitude, while the shown Canny call leaves its default L1 magnitude option unchanged.')
add(1014,'harris-matrix','Harris window matrix','Which construction produces the displayed Harris matrix A?',[
('Form products of image gradients, then average those products with a local window.','The entries are windowed I_u², I_u I_v and I_v².'),('Average raw image intensities into a scalar and duplicate it into every matrix entry.','That loses the directional gradient structure.'),('Use Gaussian derivative kernels alone without applying them to the image.','The matrix uses image gradients, not kernels by themselves.'),('Put the global maximum and minimum RGB channel values on the diagonal.','These are not the displayed local derivative products.')], 'Image derivatives may be computed by derivative-of-Gaussian filtering, but the structure matrix is built from the resulting image-gradient products.',kind='equation',conflict='The source calls I_u and I_v derivatives of a Gaussian; in the matrix they must be image gradients obtained using suitable derivative filters.',formulas=[F_HARRIS])
add(1014,'harris-eigen-cases','Flat, edge and corner cases','For the local gradient matrix, which eigenvalue pattern indicates a corner rather than a flat patch or a single edge?',[
('Both eigenvalues are substantial.','There is intensity variation in two independent directions.'),('Both eigenvalues are near zero.','That is the flat-patch case.'),('One eigenvalue is large and the other near zero.','That is the single-edge case.'),('Both eigenvalues must be negative.','A gradient-product matrix is positive semidefinite under nonnegative weighting.')], 'A corner produces significant variation in both local directions, unlike a flat region or one dominant edge.',typ='diagram',locator='Flat/edge/corner illustrations and eigenvalue interpretations.')
add(1014,'harris-response','Harris response calculation','For A=diag(4,4) and α=0.04, what is C=det(A)−α tr(A)²?',[
('13.44.','16−0.04×64=13.44.'),('15.68.','This subtracts α times the trace rather than its square.'),('−2.56.','This omits the determinant term.'),('16.00.','This omits the trace penalty.')], 'The determinant is 16 and the trace is 8, so the response is 16−2.56=13.44.',typ='numerical',difficulty='medium',steps=['Compute det(A)=4×4=16.','Compute tr(A)=8 and its square 64.','Subtract 0.04×64 from 16.'],formulas=[F_HARRIS],assumptions=['The problem supplies α=0.04; it is not inferred from an unstated source setting.'])
add([1014,1018],'harris-scale','Harris scale qualification','Which statement corrects the source summary’s grouping of Harris and SIFT as scale-invariant methods?',[
('Ordinary fixed-scale Harris is not inherently scale invariant; SIFT explicitly searches scale space.','The algorithms have different scale treatment.'),('Both methods are exactly invariant to every camera viewpoint and illumination change.','This is an even stronger unsupported guarantee.'),('Harris becomes scale invariant merely because its matrix has two eigenvalues.','Eigenvalue count does not perform scale selection.'),('SIFT avoids scale space and uses only a fixed-size Harris window.','That contradicts the displayed SIFT construction.')], 'Harris corners at a fixed scale can disappear or change character when the image scale changes. SIFT incorporates characteristic-scale selection.',conflict='The summary jointly describes Harris and SIFT as robust to scale changes without distinguishing fixed-scale Harris.')
add(1015,'dog-log','Difference of Gaussians','What is the correct relationship motivating Difference of Gaussians in scale-space detection?',[
('For nearby scales with a fixed ratio, DoG approximates a scaled version of the scale-normalized Laplacian of Gaussian.','This is an approximation with a scale factor, not exact identity.'),('DoG is exactly equal to the unnormalized LoG for every pair of arbitrary scales.','The approximation and scale normalization are missing.'),('DoG is obtained by dividing an image by its global mean intensity.','It subtracts differently Gaussian-smoothed images.'),('DoG removes the scale coordinate so all extrema must occur in one image plane.','Its extrema are searched across position and scale.')], 'Subtracting nearby Gaussian scale levels approximates a proportional scale-normalized LoG response, which motivates efficient scale-space extrema detection.',kind='equation',conflict='The slide calls DoG an approximation to LoG without explicitly stating the scale-normalized proportional relationship.')
add(1015,'neighbor-count','Scale-space extrema neighborhood','Why is a candidate DoG sample compared with 26 neighbors?',[
('Eight neighbors in its own level and nine in each adjacent scale level.','8+9+9=26, excluding the candidate itself.'),('Nine neighbors in each of three levels plus the candidate again.','That counts 28 including an extra candidate.'),('Thirteen neighbors at the same pixel in each of two levels.','This is not the spatial-scale neighborhood.'),('Only the 26 strongest pixels anywhere in the image.','The comparison is local in position and scale.')], 'The local 3×3×3 neighborhood has 27 samples, one of which is the candidate; 26 remain.',typ='diagram',locator='Three stacked DoG grids and neighboring sample diagram.')
add(1015,'scale-candidates','Scale extrema and stability','A point is a local maximum or minimum in the 3D DoG neighborhood. What follows in the SIFT pipeline?',[
('It is a candidate that still needs localization and stability tests.','Extremum detection is not the final accepted descriptor.'),('It is automatically an object identity with a correct class label.','A keypoint candidate is not semantic recognition.'),('It needs no rejection of low-contrast or edge-like responses.','Localization/stability filtering remains relevant.'),('It is guaranteed to match a unique point in every other image.','Correspondence is a later task and is not universally unique.')], 'Scale-space extrema supply candidates, followed by localization and removal of unstable responses.',typ='conceptual')
add(1016,'sift-stages','SIFT pipeline','Which sequence matches the displayed SIFT stages?',[
('Scale-space extrema → keypoint localization → orientation assignment → descriptor construction.','This is the given four-stage progression.'),('Descriptor construction → class labels → color threshold → orientation assignment.','This is not the source pipeline.'),('Orientation assignment → global histogram equalization → object masks → extrema.','Several operations and the order are unrelated to the displayed sequence.'),('Scale-space extrema → direct robot torque computation → object masks → descriptors.','The vision pipeline does not directly specify robot torques.')], 'SIFT finds stable position/scale candidates, assigns local orientation and then describes local image structure.',typ='match')
add(1016,'sift-dimensions','Descriptor dimensions','The lecture’s SIFT descriptor uses a 16×16 patch divided into 4×4 spatial cells, with 8 orientation bins per cell. What is the descriptor length?',[
('128.','There are 16 spatial cells, each contributing 8 values.'),('32.','This incorrectly uses four cells rather than 4×4 cells.'),('256.','This counts patch pixels instead of histogram entries.'),('2048.','This assigns eight bins to each pixel rather than each cell.')], 'The descriptor contains 4×4×8=128 histogram values.',typ='numerical',steps=['Count 4×4=16 spatial cells.','Multiply 16 by 8 orientation bins.'])
add(1016,'orientation-descriptor','Orientation alignment and bins','Which distinction avoids confusing SIFT’s orientation assignment with its descriptor histograms?',[
('The descriptor has eight bins per cell; that does not imply the separate dominant-orientation histogram also uses eight bins.','The two stages have different purposes and may use different binning.'),('Eight descriptor bins mean that every keypoint has exactly eight physical orientations.','Bins quantize a histogram, not the count of true orientations.'),('Orientation alignment makes descriptors independent of all viewpoint distortion.','Rotation normalization is not arbitrary projective invariance.'),('A 128-value descriptor is a 128-class object prediction.','Descriptor dimensions are not semantic class labels.')], 'A local reference orientation supports rotation robustness; its assignment must not be conflated with the later eight-bin spatial histograms.')
add(1017,'feature-filter','Strength filter semantics','The documented filter keeps strength≥0.5×maximum for percentstrength=50, then scale≥5 for minscale=5. Features have (strength,scale): A(10,6), B(6,4), C(5,5), D(4,8). Which remain?',[
('A and C.','A and C meet both inclusive thresholds.'),('A and B.','B fails the scale threshold, while C meets both.'),('Only A, because C equals a threshold.','The declared comparisons include equality.'),('A, C and D.','D fails the strength threshold.')], 'The strength threshold is 5; A,B,C pass it, then B is removed for scale 4. This is not selecting half of the feature count.',kind='code',difficulty='medium',conflict='The slide comment suggests top 50% of keypoints, but percentstrength uses a fraction of maximum response, not a count percentile.',steps=['Compute strength threshold 0.5×10=5.','Retain A,B,C by strength.','Apply scale≥5 to retain A,C.'],assumptions=['Use the explicitly supplied documented comparisons and supplied feature measurements.'])
add(1017,'keypoint-visualization','Keypoint overlay interpretation','What do the SIFT circles and hands in the actual building-image overlay represent, compared with the Harris crosses?',[
('Keypoint scale and orientation, whereas the crosses mark point locations.','This matches the source’s scale/orientation visualization.'),('Object bounding boxes and semantic class confidence scores.','Circles/hands are not detector boxes or class probabilities.'),('Exact physical grasp radius and joint torque.','No metric grasp calibration or torque model is supplied.'),('Only the original pixel color at each location.','The overlay encodes feature attributes, not raw color.')], 'SIFT’s plotted support scale and orientation are feature attributes. They should not be read as object extents or physical units.',typ='diagram',locator='Bottom Harris cross overlay and SIFT circle/hand overlay.')
add([993,1018],'feature-choice-synthesis','Choosing features for a task','A robot needs separate colored target regions, long wall boundaries and repeatable cross-view locations. Which representation plan best matches these different needs?',[
('Region classification/instances for targets, line features for walls, and point descriptors for cross-view locations.','Each family matches the type of structure required.'),('Use only color-cluster centroids as spatial wall endpoints and cross-view descriptors.','Color centroids are not spatial boundaries or local point descriptors.'),('Use only a single global mean intensity to retain every target instance and wall.','That scalar discards the required structure.'),('Treat every line segment as a complete semantic object mask.','A segment does not supply instance pixel membership.')], 'The summary combines complementary region, line and point representations. The choice depends on the task, assumptions and the information required.',typ='scenario',difficulty='medium')
